    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-01-screen-segment">
      <title>Screen and Segment Responses</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          For each response:
          <ol>
            <li>state whether it contains analyzable mathematical justification;</li>
            <li>identify the number of defensible episodes;</li>
            <li>mark each episode complete or incomplete; and</li>
            <li>state any <xref ref="sec-episode-relations" text="custom">relation</xref> among episodes.</li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        
        <tabular valign="top">
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="90%"/>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R1</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>No.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R2</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>There is a free variable, so the solution cannot be unique.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R3</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>x1 = 3 - 2x3, x2 = 4 + x3.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R4</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>There are fewer pivots than variables, so x_3 is free.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell><p><c>Therefore, the solution is not unique.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R5</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>The last row is zero, so the system is consistent.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell><p><c>Also, the third column has no pivot, so x_3 is free.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>R6</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>I do not know.</c></p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>

      <!-- <solution>

        <p>
        <dl width="narrow">
          <li>
            <title>R1</title>
            <p>
              The response does not contain analyzable mathematical justification.
              It contains one incomplete, judgment-only episode:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title>Criterion</title>
                  <p>Missing.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>Judgment</title>
                  <p><q>No.</q></p>
                </li>
              </dl>
              There is no relation to another episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>R2</title>
            <p>
              The response contains analyzable mathematical justification and one
              complete episode:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title>Criterion</title>
                  <p><q>There is a free variable.</q></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>Judgment</title>
                  <p><q>The solution cannot be unique.</q></p>
                </li>
              </dl>
              The word <q>so</q> explicitly connects the criterion to the judgment.
              There is no relation to another episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>R3</title>
            <p>
              The response contains mathematical work but no analyzable
              justification. It reports expressions for <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>
              without using them to support a judgment. It therefore contains no
              defensible episode.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>R4</title>
            <p>
              The response contains analyzable mathematical justification and two
              complete, chained episodes.
            </p>

            <p>
              The first episode is:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title>Criterion</title>
                  <p><q>There are fewer pivots than variables.</q></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>Judgment</title>
                  <p><q><m>x_3</m> is free.</q></p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              The second episode is:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title>Criterion</title>
                  <p><q><m>x_3</m> is free.</q></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>Judgment</title>
                  <p><q>The solution is not unique.</q></p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              The judgment of the first episode becomes the criterion of the second,
              so the relation is <c>chained(1)</c>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>R5</title>
            <p>
              The response contains analyzable mathematical justification and two
              complete, independent episodes.
            </p>

            <p>
              The first episode is:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title>Criterion</title>
                  <p><q>The last row is zero.</q></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>Judgment</title>
                  <p><q>The system is consistent.</q></p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              The second episode is:
              <dl width="narrow">
                <li>
                  <title>Criterion</title>
                  <p><q>The third column has no pivot.</q></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <title>Judgment</title>
                  <p><q><m>x_3</m> is free.</q></p>
                </li>
              </dl>
            </p>

            <p>
              Neither judgment supplies the criterion for the other episode, so the
              episodes are independent.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>R6</title>
            <p>
              The response does not contain analyzable mathematical justification.
              <q>I do not know</q> is not a mathematical criterion or judgment about
              the system, so the response contains no defensible episode.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
      </solution>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Screening asks only whether a response contains analyzable justification, not
          whether it is mathematically correct. Record the structure that is present; do not
          supply the argument the reference mathematics makes obvious.
        </p>
        <dl width="wide">
          <li>
            <title>R1 — <c>No.</c></title>
            <p>
              No analyzable justification: a bare answer with no reasoning. Zero episodes;
              nothing to mark complete or relate. Screen out, preserved as a response-level
              stub.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>R2 — <c>There is a free variable, so the solution cannot be unique.</c></title>
            <p>
              Contains justification. One episode, complete (a criterion and a judgment are
              both present). No relation — a single (lone) episode.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>R3 — <c>x1 = 3 - 2x3, x2 = 4 + x3.</c></title>
            <p>
              Mathematical work is present, but it is a parametric description of the solution
              set — an operation performed on the answer, not a criterion-to-judgment
              justification. No complete episode. Because work is shown, preserve it for
              review rather than excluding it outright the way a blank would be.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>R4 — <c>There are fewer pivots than variables, so x_3 is free. Therefore, the solution is not unique.</c></title>
            <p>
              Contains justification. Two episodes, both complete, <term>chained</term>: the
              judgment of the first (<m>x_3</m> is free) is named as the criterion of the
              second. Because the student states the middle term, it is the second episode's
              criterion, not a link folded inside one episode.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>R5 — <c>The last row is zero, so the system is consistent. Also, the third column has no pivot, so x_3 is free.</c></title>
            <p>
              Contains justification. Two episodes, both complete, <term>independent</term>:
              two separate reasons are offered, and the second episode's criterion is not the
              first's judgment. <q>Also</q> signals a parallel reason, not a chain.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>R6 — <c>I do not know.</c></title>
            <p>
              No analyzable justification: a no-effort response. Zero episodes; nothing to
              mark or relate. Screen out, preserved as a response-level stub.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </solution> -->
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-01-deliverables">
    <title>Blind Pass Readiness</title>
    <p>
      You are ready for the blind episode pass when you can:
      <ul>
          <li>state the project's <xref ref="g-evidence-boundary" text="title"/> accurately;</li>
          <li>identify <m>C</m>, <m>I</m>, and <m>J</m> without strengthening the response;</li>
          <li>distinguish operations from control structures;</li>
          <li>apply link status and completeness as separate decisions;</li>
          <li>recognize common incomplete and multi-episode cases;</li>
          <li>write concise evidence-based rationales; and</li>
          <li>identify uncertainty rather than conceal it;</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>